\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We identify trained $\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure as the strongest predictor of CIVO in DreamerV3 (36/36, AUROC${=}1.000$, F1${=}1.000$). No tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments (seven interventions across four levels; pathway redundancy shifts co-adaptation to unblocked channels); in pure imagination, dual critics converge ($\rho > 0.999$), so Double Q pessimism is ineffective. The feedback architecture---MPC re-planning versus imagination-based bootstrapping---is the hypothesized structural factor. Empirical validation on MPC-based architectures (e.g., TD-MPC2) remains future work.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our analysis covers proprioceptive locomotion (DMC) and simulated manipulation (ManiSkill) but not pixel-based observations, Atari, or real-robot deployments. All experiments use DreamerV3's RSSM; whether CIVO manifests in other imagination-based architectures \citep{micheli2023iris} or under learned temporal gates remains open. Statistical power is limited (4--5 seeds) \citep{colas2018seeds}; the NVE threshold may require recalibration for different reward scales. Code: upon acceptance.
\label{sec:limitations}

\section*{Broader Impact Statement}

Silent training failures in variable-rate world-model RL pose safety risks for robotic deployment. Two safeguards address this: the NVE diagnostic ($\geq 2.0\times$, AUROC${=}1.000$, $n{=}36$) detects CIVO during training, and the frozen-embedding check eliminates it (0/16 controls). Both are specific to DreamerV3's RSSM; all experiments use simulated environments without real-robot deployment.
